  "transcript": "# Mission Government\n\n## Can mission government work?\n> Yes in theory, but only with strong political leadership, the right central structures and civil service capability.\n\nChair: Is a mission Government approach possible, given our devolution and governance history?\nAlex Thomas: Yes, but it’s hard. You need three things: clear political leadership and priorities, structures that align Departments and incentives, and the civil service capability to deliver.\nHannah Keenan: Agree. Missions need systems that break departmental silos and involve wider society—not just headline milestones on paper.\n\n\n## What went wrong with the 2024 missions\n> Missions failed because goals were unclear, governance was weak, and the centre didn’t use the spending review to back them.\n\nChair: This Government had time and majority—what went wrong?\nAlex Thomas: They didn’t boil missions down to a clear theory of change, didn’t give them political energy after the election, and the civil service didn’t get them to grip the system.\nHannah Keenan: The 'Plan for Change' set milestones but mixed inputs, outputs and outcomes confusingly, and didn’t change how Departments work together.\nPeter Lamb: Shouldn’t a functional civil service bring answers on how to make cross-government things happen, not reasons why they can't?\nAlex Thomas: Yes. The civil service should ‘lean in’ with structural solutions rather than defaulting to passive replies about limits.\nHannah Keenan: A central point was missed in the 2025 spending review: it ran a conventional, departmental review, not one aligned to missions—this was the moment missions lost momentum.\n\n\n## Money, accountability and the Treasury\n> Treasury rules and departmental accounting norms resisted pooled, mission-focused budgets, undermining cross-department delivery.\n\nChair: Is the Treasury’s mindset—protecting departmental spending—part of the problem?\nHannah Keenan: Yes. Money is largely tied to Departments, so there was no effective way to fund cross-cutting missions or hold Departments horizontally accountable.\nAlex Thomas: If you mean to drive cross-department action, you can change the accounting officer model or create super-accounting arrangements—powers exist if a Government is determined.\nCharlotte Cane: Would approaches like Beyond Budgeting help, with heads of Departments working together?\nAlex Thomas: Techniques like that help, but they don’t fix the deeper incentive problem: permanent secretaries and finance directors respond to the incentives they’re given.\nSam Carling: So dedicated mission budgets would send a stronger signal?\nAlex Thomas: Yes—money plus authority would have signalled seriousness and made Departments align. Without it, missions risk being symbolic.\n\n\n## Civil service culture, capability and impartiality\n> Years of churn, crisis response and caution created passivity; impartiality can become studied neutrality if not reframed as active delivery.\n\nJohn Lamont: Do civil servants sometimes say 'that's illegal' to stop Ministers? Is this deliberate obstruction?\nHannah Keenan: Rarely deliberate. More often it's a cultural default to protect the status quo, plus hollowed-out training and capability after years of crisis work.\nAlex Thomas: Impartiality is about permanence, not passivity. The civil service must be non-partisan but also actively deliver a Government’s agenda.\nRichard Baker: Do they have the skills to implement missions?\nAlex Thomas: There are capability gaps: too much churn among policy staff reduces deep expertise and weakens delivery networks.\nHannah Keenan: We also reward moving jobs rather than building expertise, and we’re not good at removing poor performers—both hurt culture and capability.\n\n\n## Ministerial leadership and the centre of government\n> Missions needed clearer prime ministerial backing, stronger centre structures, and better-prepared Ministers who stay in post long enough to execute change.\n\nCharlotte Cane: Should Ministers get training so they can lead teams and deliver big change?\nAlex Thomas: Yes. Training, support and a pipeline for prospective Ministers helps; longer tenures also build the skill to execute policy.\nPeter Lamb: Was the centre strong enough to coordinate delivery?\nAlex Thomas: No. The UK is highly centralised but the centre is often ineffective—a small No.10, an oversized Cabinet Office, and a very powerful Treasury. That balance needs fixing.\nHannah Keenan: Mission boards being chaired by Secretaries of State was a mistake; you needed a senior centre figure to chair and hold others to account—Darren Jones only later filled part of that role.\nMarkus Campbell-Savours: How could the Prime Minister make clear who’s responsible for a mission?\nHannah Keenan: Simple but effective steps: clear, published letters of remit to Ministers, and a transparent central accountability route from the Prime Minister.\n\n\n## What would make mission government more likely to succeed?\n> Use mission budgets, a clearer centre that only does centre tasks, stronger ministerial support and civil service reform to encourage delivery.\n\nAlex Thomas: If the centre is smaller but more authoritative, if money is aligned to missions, and if ministers and officials are trained and allowed to act, missions can work.\nHannah Keenan: We need a plan to change civil service culture and capability, not just brand programmes like secondments. Small experiments help but aren’t enough.\nPeter Lamb: Would a Cabinet committee rather than mission boards have helped?\nHannah Keenan: Not necessarily—what mattered was purpose and authority: who chairs, who can make trade-offs, and where budgets sit.\nAlex Thomas: The new Office of the Prime Minister could help if it clarifies the centre’s role and supports authoritative leadership, but it’s not a cure-all.\nChair: Thank you both. The witnesses have given us things to think about—sometimes hopeful, sometimes sobering.\n",
